# Turn records for GLM-5.3-Flash on the parallel engine (--parallel above 1), kept inside the spill tier (0088). A
# record is the exact tokens the engine read and wrote for a conversation's turn, thinking included, so that a later
# request from a client that drops the thinking can be mapped back onto them (9005). It is two plain fields of the
# kept prompt state the reply ends on (decode.Snapshot): turn_key, the label and checks a lookup needs, which the tier's
# scan keeps in RAM, and turn, the body, kept only in the state's file. The tier writes, reads back and evicts a record
# with its state: there is no store, folder or eviction of its own. A turn's label names it by what a client sends back
# of it: one sha256 pass over the request's render, fed each span as length-prefixed raw bytes, reasoning left out,
# under a namespace of the tokenizer, chat template, clear_thinking, checkpoint and TF_GLM_LABEL_SALT (optional). At the
# end of an eligible reply, on the request thread and before the closing event, the reply alone continues the request's
# label pass, and the record lands on the request's own kept state unless it would replace another branch's record.
# The decoder holds a just-finished state back from early writes until its record lands (never from an eviction or the
# shutdown flush), and a new state takes the record of the state it resumed from when its prompt continues that
# record's tokens. No record for a reply the model did not end, thinking off, pictures, draft:false, ignore_eos,
# chat_template_kwargs beyond the allowed ones, several engine runs, a tool call before </think>, a call the server
# closed or cut, reasoning over 65,536 tokens or a reasoning loop: each a cache miss later, never an error. Rank 0 only;
# nothing here can fail a reply or raise into the engine; alone it changes nothing a request is served.
# mgllm, 2026-10-07.
# Tests: tests/test_9004_labels.py, tests/test_9004_records.py, tests/test_9004_kept_records.py (CPU; the first two
# need TF_GLM_TEST_MODEL_DIR).

